;; bootstrap-tooling: the --backend seed spawn route of the bootstrap period (ADR 0365); the final selfhost links the seed in-process.
(ns kotoba.compiler.nbb.seed-backend
  "BOOTSTRAP-LABELLED (ADR 0365): the opt-in `--backend seed` of the native
  AArch64 compile, on the nbb/Node host.

  After the frontend has produced the sealed KIR (`ir/native-program kir`, the
  value the artifact's `:program` holds and the verifier re-emits from), the
  driver may hand that KIR to the selfhost SEED's backend instead of
  `kotoba.native.aarch64/emit-program` (machine_ir):

      <seed> compile-kir <work>/program.kir --output <work>/program.kseed [--metered]

  The seed is the packaged seed command (scripts/seed/package.sh: the seed's
  code embedded in the C loader, wire-35 scope baked in, no spawn grant). It is
  started through the process/spawn capability's mechanism (catalog wire 20:
  no shell, an explicit argv, an empty environment), with a grant of exactly
  ONE program: a private copy of the seed whose bytes were hashed before they
  were written, so the sha256 recorded in the artifact is the sha256 of the
  program that ran. This is the bootstrap period's route only. In the final
  selfhost the seed backend is linked into the amu image as Kotoba modules and
  called in-process; nothing here has a Kotoba reading, and the namespace is
  required only under `#?(:cljs ...)`.

  Selection is fail-safe: ANY refusal (the seed's `E....` codes, e.g. E1203 /
  E2101 for floats, variants, heterogeneous vectors, documents under
  `--metered`) or failure (non-zero exit without a code, unreadable container,
  a missing export) falls back to machine_ir, and the reason goes into the
  compile report under `:backend`. A fallback artifact is the default
  artifact: it carries no `:emitter` field.

  When the seed's output is used the artifact records
  `:emitter {:name :kotoba-seed :sha256 <hex of the seed command> :route
  :bootstrap-process}` (kotoba-verifier ADR 0052). The verifier then re-emits
  with the recorded emitter, which this namespace supplies as
  `verifier/*emitters*` (see `emitters-for`).

  Container (seed/50-out.kotoba): \"KSEED1 <code-length> <n-exports>\\n\", n
  lines \"<name> <offset> <arity>\\n\", \"\\n\", then exactly <code-length> code
  bytes. The seed reports no per-function length, so a seed export is
  `{:offset o :arity a}` (machine_ir's also has `:length`; nothing in amu reads
  it)."
  {:kotoba/export []}
  )


